@@ -69,6 +69,29 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Use :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` to pretty print a JSON document supplied as a string. The output is the JSON re-serialized over multiple lines, with syntax highlighting (disable it with ``highlight=False``) and an indent of 4 spaces by default (set another width with the ``indent`` argument)::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The same functionality is available as a renderable :class:`~rich.json.JSON`, which you can pass to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('{"name": "Rich"}'))
+
+Rich also provides a package-level :func:`rich.print_json` function, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('{"name": "Rich"}')
+
+The JSON renderable disables word wrapping, so long lines are not wrapped. Printing with default options still crops lines that are wider than the console. Enable soft wrapping (``soft_wrap=True``) on :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` to keep such lines whole.
+
+JSON can also be pretty printed from the command line by running the ``rich.json`` module with the path to a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
